# ============================================================================
# CONSENSUS, DECOMPOSED
# Why a chain of self-amending law barely needs a consensus algorithm -
# and can prove exactly where it still does
# ============================================================================
#
# A standalone tutorial. No prerequisites; if you enjoy it, the nomic
# series in this repository builds the full constitutional machinery.
#
# THE DEBATE THIS FILE ANSWERS
# Proof-of-work and proof-of-stake bundle FOUR jobs into one
# resource-weighted mechanism - deciding VALIDITY, agreeing on ORDER,
# reaching FINALITY, and resisting SYBILS - and pay for all four with a
# scarce resource (energy or capital). So a single resource weights all
# four decisions at once. But the four need not travel together, and two
# of them barely need the mechanism at all. Validity is already
# DETERMINISTIC: every full node re-checks it for free, no stake
# required - unpaid Bitcoin full nodes already do exactly this. And
# compatible growth needs no ordering whatsoever. When the law is
# written in a decidable algebra, this file MEASURES what is actually
# left: only provably-conflicting moments need ordering, and finality
# becomes an entailment. What it does NOT shrink it names honestly in
# section 7 - the Sybil root, the network's own impossibility limits
# (FLP/CAP), and the real BFT/DAG cost of ordering the residue all stay.
# Consensus is not abolished here. It is unbundled, shrunk, and located.
#
# The decomposition, each line backed by a runnable proof below:
#
#   validity            computed, not voted          (the whole file)
#   compatible growth   needs NO ordering at all     (sections 1-2)
#   conflicting growth  detected, sent to the vote   (section 3)
#   ratchet moments     ordering as a ceremony       (section 4)
#   which pairs?        decidable, per case          (sections 3-4)
#   finality            an entailment, not a habit   (section 5)
#   who validates?      a governed, guarded object   (section 6)
#   sybil roots, votes  stay human - named, small    (section 7)
#
# Run me:  tau -q < consensus_decomposed.tau
# Verified against: Tau 0.7.0-alpha, build 240788e (alpha syntax may drift).
# Output guide: one-off results appear as "%N: T/F" lines; the run at
# the end prints "o0res[t] := CODE" verdicts (scan past the big formula).
# ============================================================================

# EXPECTED-RESULTS: T T F T F F T F F T T F T F
# EXPECTED-CODES: 0,9,9,5,3,8

set charvar off

# ============================================================================
# 1. ORDINARY LAWMAKING IS A CRDT - proved, not benchmarked
# ============================================================================
# A CRDT is a data structure that replicas can update independently and
# still converge, no coordinator needed. The mathematical requirement:
# updates must commute and be idempotent. For law that grows by
# intersection (new law = old law AND amendment), both are THEOREMS -
# quantified over ALL constitutions C and ALL amendments A, B. Not
# tested on examples. Proved over the infinite space.

# Order does not matter - receiving A then B equals receiving B then A:
normalize (all C:tau all A:tau all B:tau (((C & A) & B) = ((C & B) & A))).
# => T   Gossip in any order: same law. No leader, no blocks, no fees.

# Duplicates do not matter - a sybil flood delivers nothing new:
normalize (all C:tau all A:tau (((C & A) & A) = (C & A))).
# => T   A thousand copies of one amendment ARE one amendment.

# Control - so you can see the method can say no. "Adding B never
# changes anything" would make lawmaking trivial. It is false:
normalize (all C:tau all A:tau all B:tau (((C & A) & B) = (C & A))).
# => F   Amendments do change the law. Only the ORDER is irrelevant.

# ============================================================================
# 2. TWO NODES, TWO ORDERS, ONE LAW - the instance you can touch
# ============================================================================
# Node one receives a core and three amendments as P1, P2, P3.
# Node two receives the same four in the order P3, P1, P2.
# Are their constitutions equal? One decidable question:

normalize (((({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o3[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 }) & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 }) & { o7[t]=1 -> o8[t]=1 }) = ((({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o7[t]=1 -> o8[t]=1 }) & { o3[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1 }) & { o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1 })).
# => T   And note what this line IS: two replicas reconciling is itself
#        just an equality query. Convergence checking costs one verdict.

# ============================================================================
# 3. THE HONEST EXCEPTION - conflicting proposals do not commute
# ============================================================================
# Sections 1-2 hold for law that ACCEPTS both amendments. Real machines
# guard adoption: a proposal only enters if it is compatible with the
# law in force. And guarded adoption is order-dependent the moment two
# proposals contradict EACH OTHER: the node that hears P first rejects
# Q; the node that hears Q first rejects P. Two nodes, two laws. We
# falsified our own naive claim this way - and the falsification is
# itself decidable, three queries against the current core:

normalize (({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o30[t]=1 }) = 0).
# => F    proposal P alone: admissible

normalize (({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o30[t]=0 }) = 0).
# => F    proposal Q alone: admissible

normalize ((({ o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 } & { o30[t]=1 }) & { o30[t]=0 }) = 0).
# => T    together: impossible. THIS pair needs ordering - or a vote.
#
# And notice what the exception IS: two lawful proposals that exclude
# each other is not a networking problem. It is a genuine political
# conflict - exactly the case constitutional practice sends to a vote
# anyway. The coordination-free lane was never going to cover politics;
# the algebra just tells you, pair by pair, where politics begins.

# ============================================================================
# 4. WHERE ORDER COUNTS BY DESIGN - the ratchet ceremony
# ============================================================================
# Not all operations commute. A SEAL (freeze the current law as a
# permanent floor) and a REPLACE (drop everything unsealed) interact:
# seal-then-replace keeps a right that replace-then-seal destroys.
# Is that order-dependence universal? Ask over ALL states and inputs.
# After seal-first the law is L & X; after replace-first it is S & X
# (L = current law, S = current floor, X = the replacement):

normalize (all L:tau all S:tau all X:tau ((L & X) = (S & X))).
# => F   Not universally equal: ratchet moments genuinely need ordering.
#        THIS is the consensus residue - rare, constitutional, worth
#        a heavyweight ceremony. Everything else rides the CRDT lane.

# But "SEAL and REPLACE conflict" is a rule about OPERATION TYPES.
# The algebra can do better: it decides commutation PER CASE.
# Floor { o10=0 }, law additionally carries an unsealed right o12.
# Replacement X = { o14=1 } does not re-add o12 - order matters:

normalize (({ o10[t]=0 && o12[t]=1 } & { o14[t]=1 }) = ({ o10[t]=0 } & { o14[t]=1 })).
# => F   Conflict detected: this pair goes to the ordering ceremony.

# Same state, but the replacement itself carries o12 along:
normalize (({ o10[t]=0 && o12[t]=1 } & { o12[t]=1 && o14[t]=1 }) = ({ o10[t]=0 } & { o12[t]=1 && o14[t]=1 })).
# => T   Same operation TYPES - yet provably order-free, because the
#        replacement re-adds the unsealed content. A type-based
#        scheduler would serialize this; the algebra proves it need
#        not. Consensus only where order PROVABLY matters, decided
#        case by case, one verdict per pair.

# ============================================================================
# 5. FINALITY AS ENTAILMENT - reorg protection you can compute
# ============================================================================
# Chains treat finality as a social convention ("we do not reorg that
# deep") or an economic threat. Here a sealed checkpoint gives a harder
# currency: a fork is acceptable if and only if its law still ENTAILS
# every sealed checkpoint. Checkpoint: { o10=0 && o16=1 }.

# Fork one grew new law o18 and still carries the checkpoint:
normalize ((({ o10[t]=0 && o16[t]=1 && o18[t]=1 }) & ({ o10[t]=0 && o16[t]=1 })) = ({ o10[t]=0 && o16[t]=1 && o18[t]=1 })).
# => T   Valid fork - it contains the sealed past.

# Fork two "reorganized away" the sealed right o16:
normalize ((({ o10[t]=0 && o18[t]=1 }) & ({ o10[t]=0 && o16[t]=1 })) = ({ o10[t]=0 && o18[t]=1 })).
# => F   Rejected - not by node policy, by an empty intersection.
#        Rolling back a sealed checkpoint is not rude. It is false.
#
# One honest dependency: this reduces finality to "does the fork keep the
# seal" - but agreeing WHICH state was sealed is itself the rare ordering
# ceremony of section 4. Finality is not free; it is an entailment
# RELATIVE TO an already-ordered seal. What section 5 buys is only that,
# once a checkpoint is sealed, no further social "we don't reorg that
# deep" convention is needed - the intersection decides the rest.

# ============================================================================
# 6. WHO VALIDATES? - the validator set as a governed object
# ============================================================================
# The residue that DOES need ordering needs somebody to order it - and
# "who chooses the choosers?" is where delegated systems rot into
# oligarchy. Here the rotation rule itself can be entrenched law:
# validator change o60 requires rotation proof o61. Guarantee, over
# EVERY amendment X that any future governance could ever adopt:

normalize (all X:tau (((({ o60[t]=1 -> o61[t]=1 } & X) & { o60[t]=1 && o61[t]=0 })) = 0)).
# => T   No amendment path ever legalizes rotation-free capture.

# Counter-probe - without the entrenched rule the guarantee must fail:
normalize (all X:tau (((X & { o60[t]=1 && o61[t]=0 })) = 0)).
# => F   (X can simply permit it.) The protection is the entrenchment,
#        not wishful quantification - and the machine knows the difference.
#
# The double edge (as wherever the ratchet appears): this same guarantee
# preserves a CAPTURED validator set exactly as faithfully as a fair one.
# The algebra proves no amendment escapes the entrenched rule; it cannot
# tell a just first seal from an unjust one. Legitimacy of the initial
# validator set is a social fact, external to the machine - preservation,
# not wisdom.

# ============================================================================
# 7. THE HONEST RESIDUE - what no algebra buys you
# ============================================================================
# - Governance, not payments: this whole decomposition is about LAW that
#   grows by intersection (meet). Value transfer is a different animal -
#   a token spend is neither idempotent nor commutative (double-spend) -
#   so payments do NOT ride the CRDT lane. The detector PRINCIPLE still
#   generalizes (order only non-commuting pairs = the same-account rule
#   of DAG chains like Avalanche), but a payment layer needs its own
#   conflict relation, not this one.
# - Networks stay hard: the ordering ceremony still needs a real BFT or
#   DAG protocol, with its timing assumptions. The algebra shrinks WHAT
#   needs agreement, not the difficulty of agreeing over an unreliable
#   network.
# - The sybil ROOT stays external: whether a key is one person or one
#   datacenter, no algebra can see. Weigh the residual, genuine value
#   choices by persons - the one place counting belongs.
# - Where order matters, ordering power exists (front-running in
#   miniature). The surface is small and public - but not zero.
# - "Every node computes the same verdict" presupposes every node runs
#   the SAME decision procedure. Different implementations that
#   disagree on one verdict fork the chain on validity itself - so a
#   verdict-level reference semantics is consensus-critical
#   infrastructure, not a nicety.
# - Computing a verdict costs work, and some inputs are crafted to be
#   expensive. A verdict chain needs a decidable cost bound per
#   proposal - size caps play the role that gas plays elsewhere.
#ja
# ============================================================================
# 8. THE QUIET CHAIN - one node's day, live
# ============================================================================
# The machine below is a sealed-floor ratchet (two tau state streams).
# Inputs per step: i1 = a proposal (a specification), i2 = the mode:
# 0 ADD, 1 SEAL, 2 REPLACE. Verdict codes: 9 adopted, 5 sealed,
# 3 replaced, 8 rejected. Step zero is initialization (code 0).
#
# step 1  ADD  fee rule           -> 9   CRDT lane: any arrival order
# step 2  ADD  reserve rule       -> 9   would yield this same law
# step 3  SEAL                    -> 5   the one ordered ceremony:
#                                        current law becomes the floor
# step 4  REPLACE with new rule   -> 3   unsealed law swapped freely -
#                                        the floor rides along
# step 5  REPLACE breaking floor  -> 8   the "deep reorg": rejected as
#                                        an empty intersection, not as
#                                        a policy violation
#
# NOTE: after `run`, every following line is consumed as input
# (i1, then i2, per step) - no blank lines from here to end of file.

i1 : tau := in console
i2 : bv[8] := in console

run ( (o0seal[0]:tau = { o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 }) && (o0law[0]:tau = { o1[t]=1 -> o2[t]=1 }) && ( (i2[t]:bv[8] = { #x01 }:bv[8]) ? ((o0seal[t]:tau = o0law[t-1]:tau) && (o0law[t]:tau = o0law[t-1]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x05 }:bv[8])) : ( (o0seal[t]:tau = o0seal[t-1]:tau) && ( (i2[t]:bv[8] = { #x02 }:bv[8]) ? ( ((o0seal[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) != 0) ? ((o0law[t]:tau = o0seal[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x03 }:bv[8])) : ((o0law[t]:tau = o0law[t-1]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8])) ) : ( ((o0law[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) != 0) ? ((o0law[t]:tau = o0law[t-1]:tau & i1[t]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x09 }:bv[8])) : ((o0law[t]:tau = o0law[t-1]:tau) && (o0res[t]:bv[8] = { #x08 }:bv[8])) ) ) ) ) )
o3[t]=1 -> o4[t]=1
0
o5[t]=1 -> o6[t]=1
0
o7[t]=1 -> o8[t]=1
1
o20[t]=1
2
o1[t]=1 && o2[t]=0
2
